@node REQUIREMENTS "What you need"

@{b}On the Amiga@{ub}

  AmigaOS 3.0 or newer.

  A TCP/IP stack offering @{b}bsdsocket.library@{ub}. Roadshow, AmiTCP and
  Miami all work. Under UAE the built-in bsdsocket emulation works, and
  needs no configuration beyond switching it on.

  @{b}AmiSSL 5@{ub} only if you connect over @{link "TLS" TLS}. Plain http
  needs nothing extra, and AmiSSL is opened only when it is actually asked
  for -- an installation without it runs perfectly well.

  @{b}MUI 3.8@{ub} or newer, for the dashboard. The command line modes
  work without it.

  A 68020 is comfortable. A 68000 build is included and works, but JSON
  over TCP on a stock A500 is slow.

  Memory follows the size of your @{i}dashboard@{ui}, not the size of your
  Home Assistant. A dashboard of a dozen entities costs a couple of KB
  even if the server has two thousand.

@{b}On the Home Assistant side@{ub}

  Any reasonably recent version. You need to be able to log in and reach
  your profile page, which is where the access token comes from.

  ami2ha talks plain HTTP to the WebSocket API, so the Amiga and Home
  Assistant should be on the same network. See @{"When it breaks" link TROUBLE}
  if yours is behind HTTPS.

@endnode

@node INSTALL "Installing"

@{b}With the installer@{ub}

  Double-click the @{b}Install@{ub} icon.

  From a Shell you have to name the Installer in full, because it is not
  on the command path:

    SYS:System/Installer Install

  On AmigaOS 3.1 and earlier it lives in @{b}SYS:Utilities@{ub} instead.

  It asks where to put things, copies the build that matches your
  processor, and creates an icon you can double-click.

@{b}By hand@{ub}

  It is only one program. Copy it wherever you like:

    MakeDir Work:ami2ha
    Copy ami2ha.020 Work:ami2ha/ami2ha
    Copy ami2ha.guide Work:ami2ha
    Copy icons/ami2ha.info Work:ami2ha

  Use @{b}ami2ha@{ub} (the 68000 build) instead if you are not on an
  020 or better.

  Then read @{"The token" link TOKEN}, which is the part everyone has to
  do.

@endnode

@node TOKEN "The token"

ami2ha logs in with a @{b}long-lived access token@{ub}. This is a long
string of letters and digits that Home Assistant generates for you, and
it stands in for your password.

@{b}Getting one@{ub}

  1. Open Home Assistant in a browser on any machine.
  2. Click your user name, bottom left.
  3. Go to the @{b}Security@{ub} tab.
  4. Scroll to @{b}Long-lived access tokens@{ub} and create one.
     Call it "amiga" so you can recognise it later.
  5. Copy the token @{i}now@{ui}. Home Assistant shows it once and never
     again.

@{b}Putting it on the Amiga@{ub}

  Save it to a file, on its own, with nothing else in it:

    Work:ami2ha/ha.token

  Any text editor will do. Trailing newlines are fine.

@{b}Keep it to yourself@{ub}

  That token can control everything in your house. Treat the file the way
  you would treat a password:

  - Do not put it on a shared drive or in a public archive.
  - Do not pass it with the TOKEN argument on the command line, where it
    would sit in your shell history. Use TOKENFILE, which is what it is
    for.
  - It travels over your network in the clear, which is fine on your own
    LAN and not fine over the internet.

  If it ever leaks, delete it in Home Assistant and make a new one. The
  old one stops working immediately.

@endnode

@node LABEL "Choosing entities"

A full Home Assistant has hundreds or thousands of entities. You want a
dozen. There are two ways to say which.

@{b}The easy way: a label@{ub}

  In Home Assistant, create a label called @{b}amiga@{ub} and apply it to
  the entities you want on the Amiga.

    Settings -> Areas, labels & zones -> Labels -> Create label

  Then open an entity, click the settings cog, and add the label under
  @{b}Advanced settings@{ub}. Do that for each entity you want.

  Tell ami2ha to use it, and it asks the server which entities carry that
  label and shows exactly those:

    ami2ha 192.168.1.100 TOKENFILE=Work:ami2ha/ha.token LABEL=amiga
             WRITECONFIG=Work:ami2ha/dash.cfg

  The nice part is that you can add and remove entities from the Amiga
  later without touching the Amiga: label something new in Home
  Assistant, restart ami2ha, and there it is.

@{b}The explicit way: list them@{ub}

  Write the entity ids into the configuration file yourself. See
  @{"The dashboard" link DASHBOARD} for the format. This suits a fixed
  display you do not intend to change.

@{b}Finding entity ids@{ub}

  To see what your server offers:

    ami2ha 192.168.1.100 TOKENFILE=Work:ami2ha/ha.token LIST

  Add DOMAIN=switch to narrow it down. Be warned that without a label
  this fetches everything, which on a large installation is a lot of
  entities and a lot of memory.

@endnode

@node FIRSTRUN "First run"

Prove the connection works before worrying about the dashboard:

  ami2ha 192.168.1.100 TOKENFILE=Work:ami2ha/ha.token LIST

Use your own server's address. If Home Assistant is on a non-standard
port, add PORT=8124.

You should get a list of entities. If you do not, go to
@{"When it breaks" link TROUBLE}.

Now make a dashboard from your labelled entities:

  ami2ha 192.168.1.100 TOKENFILE=Work:ami2ha/ha.token LABEL=amiga
           WRITECONFIG=Work:ami2ha/dash.cfg

And open it:

  ami2ha CONFIG=Work:ami2ha/dash.cfg GUI

The configuration file remembers the host and the token file, so from
here on CONFIG and GUI are all you need.

@endnode

@node EXAMPLE "A worked example"

Start to finish, with nothing assumed. Substitute your own address.

@{b}1. Get a token@{ub}

  In Home Assistant: your user name, bottom left -> Security tab ->
  Long-lived access tokens -> create one. Copy it; it is shown once.

  Save it on the Amiga, on a line of its own:

    Work:ami2ha/ha.token

@{b}2. Label what you want to see@{ub}

  In Home Assistant, make a label called @{b}amiga@{ub} and apply it to
  the entities you want. See @{"Choosing entities" link LABEL}.

@{b}3. Let ami2ha write its own configuration@{ub}

    Work:ami2ha/ami2ha 192.168.1.100 TOKENFILE=Work:ami2ha/ha.token
             LABEL=amiga WRITECONFIG=Work:ami2ha/ami2ha.cfg

  It connects, asks which entities carry the label, and writes:

    host       192.168.1.100
    tokenfile  Work:ami2ha/ha.token
    label      amiga

    group "amiga"
        sensor sensor.outside_temperature label "Outside"
        toggle switch.desk_lamp label "Desk lamp"
    end

@{b}4. Run it@{ub}

    Work:ami2ha/ami2ha CONFIG=Work:ami2ha/ami2ha.cfg GUI

  That is the whole command from now on. Everything else is in the file.

@{b}5. Make it start from Workbench@{ub}

    Work:ami2ha/ami2ha CONFIG=Work:ami2ha/ami2ha.cfg WRITEICON

  Now double-clicking the icon does the same thing.

@{b}What goes where@{ub}

  This trips people up, so plainly:

  @{b}The configuration file@{ub} holds everything -- the address, the
  port, which token file to read, the label, and the entire dashboard
  layout. It is the file you keep.

  @{b}The tool types@{ub} in the icon are only how a Workbench launch
  passes arguments, because there is no command line to put them on.
  In practice you want exactly one:

    CONFIG=Work:ami2ha/ami2ha.cfg

  HOST and TOKENFILE also exist as tool types, and as command line
  arguments, because anything given that way @{i}overrides@{ui} the
  configuration file. That is useful for pointing a test run at another
  server without editing anything. It is not how you configure the
  program day to day, and putting your settings in both places is the
  quickest way to confuse yourself about which one is being used.

@endnode

@node DASHBOARD "The dashboard"

The window is built from a configuration file. You can edit it in the
@{"settings window" link SETTINGS} or in a text editor; both write the
same format.

@{b}The file@{ub}

  host       192.168.1.100
  port       8123
  tokenfile  Work:ami2ha/ha.token
  label      amiga

  group "Living room"
      sensor sensor.living_room_temperature label "Temperature"
      gauge  sensor.humidity label "Humidity" min 0 max 100
      toggle switch.lamp label "Lamp"
      button script.goodnight label "Goodnight" service script.turn_on
      text   "Anything you like"
  end

@{b}What the kinds do@{ub}

  @{b}sensor@{ub}  a reading, with its unit
  @{b}gauge@{ub}   a reading, as a bar; min and max set the range
  @{b}toggle@{ub}  a checkmark that switches the entity
  @{b}button@{ub}  runs a service when clicked
  @{b}text@{ub}    a caption; shows nothing from the server

@{b}Groups@{ub}

  Every widget lives in a group, and each group is drawn as a titled box.
  Groups are yours to arrange: they need not match the areas in Home
  Assistant, and an entity can go wherever it makes sense to you.

@{b}The status line@{ub}

  Along the bottom. Normally the server version and how many entities are
  loaded. It also reports what the program is doing -- switching
  something, or trying to reconnect.

@endnode

@node SETTINGS "Settings"

@{b}Project -> Settings@{ub}, or right-Amiga S.

  @{b}Group@{ub}       which group you are editing. New group and Delete
                group work on this one; Name renames it.

  @{b}Available HA entities@{ub}  everything the server offers that is not
                on the dashboard yet.

  @{b}Selected entities@{ub}      what the current group shows, in order.
                Drag to reorder, or use Up and Down.

  @{b}Add and Remove@{ub}  move an entity between the two lists. To move
                one to a different group: remove it here, switch group
                above, and add it there. It keeps its label and its
                settings on the way.

  @{b}Properties@{ub}   the selected entity's label, how it is shown, and
                the range for a gauge. Changes are kept when you move to
                another entity; you do not have to press Return.

  @{b}Save@{ub} writes the file and closes. @{b}Use@{ub} applies without
  writing, so it is gone at the next start. @{b}Cancel@{ub} puts
  everything back.

@endnode

@node STARTING "Starting it"

@{b}From a Shell@{ub}

  ami2ha CONFIG=Work:ami2ha/dash.cfg GUI

  Add it to your @{b}user-startup@{ub} to have it come up with the
  machine:

    Run >NIL: Work:ami2ha/ami2ha CONFIG=Work:ami2ha/dash.cfg GUI

@{b}From Workbench@{ub}

  Double-clicking the icon needs the settings in the icon instead of on a
  command line. ami2ha can write that icon for you:

    ami2ha CONFIG=Work:ami2ha/dash.cfg WRITEICON

  That creates ami2ha.info with the right tool types. From then on
  double-clicking is enough.

  To change them later, use Workbench's @{b}Information@{ub} on the icon
  and edit the tool types by hand:

    CONFIG=Work:ami2ha/dash.cfg
    HOST=192.168.1.100
    TOKENFILE=Work:ami2ha/ha.token

@{b}Arguments@{ub}

  HOST PORT/N TOKEN/K TOKENFILE/K CONFIG/K WRITECONFIG/K GUI/S LIST/S
  WATCH/S WRITEICON/S GET/K TOGGLE/K ON/K OFF/K DOMAIN/K TIMEOUT/N/K

@endnode

@node AREXX "ARexx"

ami2ha hosts an ARexx port called @{b}AMI2HA@{ub}, so the rest of your
Workbench can read your house and control it. A second copy takes
AMI2HA.1, and so on.

  /* every script must start with a comment */
  OPTIONS RESULTS
  ADDRESS AMI2HA

  GET sensor.living_room_temperature
  SAY 'It is' RESULT 'degrees'

  ON switch.lamp
  IF RC ~= 0 THEN DO
      LASTERROR
      SAY 'failed:' RESULT
  END

@{b}Commands@{ub}

  GET <id>                  the state, as a string
  ATTR <id> <attribute>     one attribute
  LIST [DOMAIN <domain>]    known entity ids, one per line
  TOGGLE <id>               flip it
  ON <id> / OFF <id>        set it
  CALL <domain> <service> [ENTITY <id>] [DATA <json>]
  STATUS                    connection state, version, entity count
  COUNT                     how many entities are held
  LASTERROR                 why the last command failed
  VERSION                   the ami2ha version
  QUIT                      close the program

RC is 0 on success, 5 when the request made sense but could not be
answered, 10 when it did not parse.

ARexx puts unquoted words into upper case, and Home Assistant ids are
lower case; ami2ha folds them back, so you need not quote ids. Do quote
the JSON passed to DATA, whose keys are case sensitive.

The full description, including the parts that are still only designed,
is in AREXX.md in the source archive.

@endnode

@node TROUBLE "When it breaks"

@{b}"cannot open bsdsocket.library"@{ub}

  No TCP/IP stack is running. Start Roadshow, AmiTCP or Miami first.
  Under UAE, switch on bsdsocket emulation in the configuration.

@{b}"amisslmaster.library not found -- AmiSSL is not installed"@{ub}

  TLS was asked for but AmiSSL is not on this machine. Install AmiSSL 5,
  or drop the TLS switch and connect over plain http.

@{b}"the server's certificate was not accepted"@{ub}

  The message after it says which check failed. A self-signed certificate
  gives "self-signed certificate"; a name that does not match what you
  typed gives "Hostname mismatch" -- reach the server by the name on its
  certificate rather than by its IP address. See @{link "TLS" TLS} for the
  NOVERIFY escape hatch and what it gives up.

@{b}TLS fails, but the same address works in a browser@{ub}

  Two things bite here, and neither is obvious.

  A reverse proxy usually chooses its certificate from the name you asked
  for, and many refuse a connection made to a bare IP address because no
  name is sent at all. Use the host name, not the number.

  But AmigaOS has no mDNS, so a name ending in .local -- which is what
  Home Assistant suggests by default -- does not resolve here even though
  every other machine in the house finds it. Give it an address by hand in
  @{b}DEVS:Internet/hosts@{ub}:

    192.168.1.100    homeassistant.local

  That one line is enough, and it is also what lets the name on the
  certificate match what you connected to.

@{b}"could not connect"@{ub}

  The address or the port is wrong, or the machine cannot be reached.
  Check with a Ping if you have one. Remember Home Assistant listens on
  8123 unless you changed it.

  ami2ha keeps trying, waiting a little longer each time up to a minute,
  and says so in the status line. It does not need restarting when your
  server comes back.

@{b}"Token rejected"@{ub}

  The token is wrong, expired, or was deleted in Home Assistant. Make a
  new one -- see @{"The token" link TOKEN}. ami2ha deliberately does
  @{i}not@{ui} retry this one, because Home Assistant bans an address
  after repeated failed logins and a retry loop would lock your Amiga
  out.

@{b}"cannot open muimaster.library"@{ub}

  MUI is not installed. The GUI needs it; LIST, GET, ON, OFF and WATCH do
  not.

@{b}Readings show a dash@{ub}

  The server reports that entity as unavailable or unknown. That is
  usually the device, not ami2ha -- check the same entity in Home
  Assistant.

@{b}HTTPS@{ub}

  Not supported yet. If your Home Assistant is only reachable over
  HTTPS, ami2ha cannot talk to it. On most home setups the server also
  answers plain HTTP on port 8123 from inside the network.

@{b}Still stuck@{ub}

  Project -> About shows which server this copy is actually talking to,
  which answers the first question more often than you would think.

  Bug reports are welcome at
  https://github.com/evil4dmin/ami2ha/issues

@endnode

@node TLS "TLS"

@{b}Connecting over TLS@{ub}

  Home Assistant serves plain http on port 8123 by default, and on your own
  network that is usually what you want. If your server is behind https --
  a reverse proxy, or Home Assistant's own SSL settings -- add the
  @{b}TLS@{ub} switch:

    ami2ha myhouse.example.com TLS TOKENFILE=Work:ami2ha/ha.token

  The port then defaults to 443 rather than 8123, so you only need to name
  a port if yours is different.

  In a configuration file the same thing is:

    host       myhouse.example.com
    tls        yes

  This needs @{b}AmiSSL 5@{ub} installed. It is opened the first time TLS
  is used and not before, so it costs nothing on a plain http setup.

@{b}Certificates@{ub}

  By default ami2ha checks that the certificate is signed by a trusted
  authority @{i}and@{ui} that it names the host you asked for. Without the
  second check any valid certificate for any site would be accepted, which
  is no protection at all.

  A self-signed certificate -- common on a home server -- fails that check.
  You can turn it off:

    ami2ha myhouse TLS NOVERIFY TOKENFILE=Work:ami2ha/ha.token

  or in the configuration file:

    tls        yes
    tlsverify  no

  Be clear about what this costs. The traffic is still encrypted, so nobody
  reading the wire sees your token. But nothing proves any more that the
  machine answering is yours, and your token is handed to whatever does
  answer. Only do this on a network you trust.

@{b}Home Assistant Cloud (Nabu Casa)@{ub}

  Remote access works, and is the case TLS is really for -- reaching your
  house from somewhere else. Use the ui.nabu.casa address as the host with
  the TLS switch and nothing else changes.

  Its certificate is a proper one, so verification can stay on. Expect a
  connection to take a few seconds longer than one on your own network:
  the cloud carries the encrypted session through to your server, so the
  handshake travels the whole way. That tunnel also drops a handshake
  occasionally; ami2ha simply tries again, and you will not normally see it.

@{b}Is it worth it?@{ub}

  On a home LAN, honestly, often not: TLS on a 68k costs real time on every
  connection, and the token never leaves your own network. It earns its
  keep when you reach the server from outside the house, which is exactly
  when the token would otherwise cross networks in clear.

@endnode

